    slug: "alaska-straubs-1914-recipe",
    name: "Alaska (Straub's 1914 recipe)",
    description: "Adapted from a recipe in Jacques Straub's 1914 book Drinks. The proportions are exactly as per his recipe, but Straub calls for his Alaska to be shaken rather than stirred. [Hence, I've added a splash of water to very subtly increase dilution.] Alaska Cocktail. 1 dash orange bitters. 1/3 jigger yellow chartreuse. 2/3 jigger Tom gin. Shake. Jacques Straub, 1914 Conjecture suggests this cocktail is so named due to its golden colour, referencing the Alaska Gold Rush, which was big news around the time of its creation. The Klondike Gold Rush, which took place in the Yukon region of Canada, started in 1896. By the summer of 1899, many of the prospectors left the Klondike for an area around Nome in west Alaska following the discovery of gold there. Interestingly, the Nome Cocktail is a",
