    id: "1ef72b2e-575a-4e6d-a0e3-a5b3a280f139",
    slug: "dirty-martini",
    name: "Dirty Martini",
    description: "Cocktail historian David Wondrich has traced the origins of the Dirty Martini back to 1901 and a bartender called John E. O'Connor, who served a Dry Martini with muddled olives at New York's Waldorf Astoria. The first written reference to brine being added to a Martini-style cocktail appears in G.H. Steele's 1930 My New Cocktail Book . PERFECT (a la Hyland ) 50% Plymouth Gin 50% French Vermouth Angostura bitters Orange bitters Peychaud bitters Olive brine (1 teaspoon) G. F. Steele, 1930 Fun fact: Franklin Delano Roosevelt, the 32nd President, a keen home bartender, is often connected to this cocktail. However, besides a story that Joseph Stalin recommended he sip pickle juice and vodka as a cure for a hangover at the Yalta Conference in 1945, there is no evidence that he used oliv",
